<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Chapter 6 exercises.

     One flat list, numbered in order. Which exercises get assignedz
     together lives in the course schedule, not here.

     Exercise 1 is new (subgame perfection, following page 1 of the
     notes). Exercises 2, 3, and 5 are adapted from the previous
     instructor's Lesson 10 written homework, in the notation of the
     Chapter 6 notes: Exercise 2 = old Exercise 1, Exercise 3 = old
     Problem 1(a)-(c), Exercise 5 = old Problem 2 plus a new subgame part.
     Exercise 4 is new: it proves tit for tat is a Nash equilibrium
     (replacing old Problem 1(d)) and rewrites old Problem 1(e) as a
     direct subgame argument. Old Exercise 2 (the unequal game) is cut.

     Add more by appending another <exercise> below; numbering of the
     existing ones will not shift. -->

<exercises xml:id="ch6-exercises">
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-spne-threat">
    <title>Subgame Perfect Equilibria Practice</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This exercise does not involve a repeated game. Consider the game
        in extensive form below, with payoffs listed as (Player 1, Player
        2). Player 1 chooses <m>U</m> or <m>D</m>, and then Player 2
        chooses <m>x</m> or <m>y</m>. In the tree, <m>P_1</m> and
        <m>P_2</m> mark which player moves at each node, and Player 2's two
        nodes are labeled <m>n_1</m> (after <m>U</m>) and <m>n_2</m> (after
        <m>D</m>).
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-hw-threat-tree">
        <caption>The game tree. Payoffs are listed as (Player 1, Player 2).</caption>
        <image source="ch6-graph/hw-threat-tree.svg" width="85%">
          <shortdescription>Game tree for the exercise</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              At the root, Player 1 chooses <m>U</m> or <m>D</m>. If Player
              1 chooses <m>U</m>, Player 2 chooses at node <m>n_1</m>:
              <m>x</m> gives payoffs <m>(2, 3)</m>, and <m>y</m> gives
              <m>(0, 1)</m>. If Player 1 chooses <m>D</m>, Player 2 chooses
              at node <m>n_2</m>: <m>x</m> gives <m>(1, 0)</m>, and
              <m>y</m> gives <m>(3, 2)</m>.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Write Player 2's strategies as two letters: the first letter is her
        move at <m>n_1</m>, and the second is her move at <m>n_2</m>. For
        example, <m>xy</m> means <q>play <m>x</m> at <m>n_1</m> and
        <m>y</m> at <m>n_2</m>.</q>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-spne-threat-normal-form">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write this game in normal form. Player 1 has two strategies, and
          Player 2 has four.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-spne-threat-nash">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>(U, xx)</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-spne-threat-subgames">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Decide whether <m>(U, xx)</m> is a subgame perfect Nash
          equilibrium. For each subgame of this game:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              write the normal form of the subgame (for the subgames at
              <m>n_1</m> and <m>n_2</m>, this is a smaller table than the
              one in part (a)),
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              box the outcome of <m>(U, xx)</m> restricted to the subgame,
              and
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              say whether the restriction is a Nash equilibrium of the
              subgame, and why.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          The whole game is one subgame, and its table is the one from part
          (a). For the subgames at <m>n_1</m> and <m>n_2</m>, notice that
          there is no <m>U</m> or <m>D</m> in the table: Player 1 doesn't
          move in these subgames, so Player 1 has no strategies there. Player
          2 has two strategies, <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, in both. So the tables
          look like this:
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Subgame at <m>n_1</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>x</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>y</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Payoffs</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Subgame at <m>n_2</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>x</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>y</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Payoffs</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          To box the outcome of <m>(U, xx)</m> in each subgame, first decide:
          what does Player 2's strategy <m>xx</m> correspond to in the
          subgame at <m>n_1</m>? In the subgame at <m>n_2</m>?
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-spne-threat-words">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use backward induction to find the subgame perfect Nash
          equilibrium of this game. Your answer should be a strategy for each
          player, not just the path the game takes: Player 2's strategy needs
          to say what she does at <em>both</em> <m>n_1</m> and <m>n_2</m>,
          because Player 1's choice depends on what Player 2 would do at
          either node, even the one that isn't reached. Then compare its
          outcome with the outcome of <m>(U, xx)</m>. Explain in words why Player 2's plan at
          <m>n_2</m> in <m>(U, xx)</m> is a threat, and who benefits from it.
          Why does the threat only work if Player 1 believes it?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-trigger-and-tit-for-tat">
    <title>Trigger and Tit for Tat</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Players 1 and 2 play the infinitely repeated game whose stage game
        is the Prisoner's Dilemma below, with payoffs listed as (Player 1,
        Player 2).
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-pd-5-8">
        <title>The stage game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>c</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>d</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>c</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(5, 5)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(-8, 8)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>d</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(8, -8)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        The players play this stage game in rounds <m>k = 0, 1, 2, \ldots</m>,
        with discount factor <m>\delta</m>, where <m>0 \lt \delta \lt 1</m>.
        If <m>p_k</m> denotes a player's payoff in round <m>k</m>, then that
        player's payoff for the repeated game is
        <me>
          \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \delta^k p_k = p_0 + \delta p_1 + \delta^2 p_2 + \cdots.
        </me>
      </p>

      <p>
        We will use the following strategies for the repeated game:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\sigma</m>, the <term>trigger</term> strategy: begin by
            playing <m>c</m>. Keep playing <m>c</m> as long as the other
            player plays <m>c</m>. If the other player ever plays <m>d</m>,
            play <m>d</m> in every round after that.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\tau</m>, <term>tit for tat</term>: begin by playing <m>c</m>.
            After that, play whatever the other player played in the
            previous round.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\nu</m>, <term>suspicious tit for tat</term>: begin by
            playing <m>d</m>. After that, play whatever the other player
            played in the previous round.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-trigger-and-tit-for-tat-words">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose Player 1 uses <m>\sigma</m> in the repeated game. Explain
          in words what this means.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-trigger-and-tit-for-tat-sigma-tau">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the payoffs <m>\pi_1(\sigma, \tau)</m> and
          <m>\pi_2(\sigma, \tau)</m> when Player 1 uses <m>\sigma</m> and
          Player 2 uses <m>\tau</m>. Make it clear in your work which move
          each player makes in each round. You can explain this in words,
          or show the pattern as part of your calculation.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-trigger-and-tit-for-tat-tau-nu">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the payoffs <m>\pi_1(\tau, \nu)</m> and
          <m>\pi_2(\tau, \nu)</m> when Player 1 uses <m>\tau</m> and Player
          2 uses <m>\nu</m>. Start by filling in the table below.
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell>round 0</cell><cell>round 1</cell><cell>round 2</cell><cell>round 3</cell><cell>round 4</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1's move</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 2's move</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1's payoff</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 2's payoff</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          The payoffs alternate in sign. A series like
          <m>1 - \delta + \delta^2 - \delta^3 + \cdots</m> is still geometric:
          it has <m>a = 1</m> and <m>r = -\delta</m>, so it equals
          <m>\frac{1}{1 - (-\delta)} = \frac{1}{1 + \delta}</m>. What are
          <m>a</m> and <m>r</m> for each player's payoffs?
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-trigger-and-tit-for-tat-threshold">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\delta_0</m> such that the strategy profile
          <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a Nash equilibrium of the repeated game
          whenever <m>\delta \geq \delta_0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-tit-for-tat-equilibrium">
    <title>Tit for Tat Against Other Strategies</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider again the repeated Prisoner's Dilemma from
        <xref ref="hw-trigger-and-tit-for-tat"/>, with the same stage game,
        the same discount factor <m>\delta</m>, and the same strategies
        <m>\sigma</m> (trigger), <m>\tau</m> (tit for tat), and
        <m>\nu</m> (suspicious tit for tat).
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-tit-for-tat-equilibrium-compare">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain how the payoffs for the strategy profile
          <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> compare with the payoffs for the strategy
          profile <m>(\tau, \tau)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-tit-for-tat-equilibrium-nu">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\delta_0</m> such that
          <m>\pi_1(\nu, \tau) \leq \pi_1(\tau, \tau)</m> whenever
          <m>\delta \geq \delta_0</m>. That is, show that for a large enough
          discount factor, Player 1 cannot improve their payoff by switching
          from <m>\tau</m> to <m>\nu</m> when Player 2 plays <m>\tau</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-tit-for-tat-equilibrium-beta">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\beta</m> denote the strategy <q>always play
          <m>d</m>.</q> Find <m>\delta_0</m> such that
          <m>\pi_1(\beta, \tau) \leq \pi_1(\tau, \tau)</m> whenever
          <m>\delta \geq \delta_0</m>. That is, show that for a large enough
          discount factor, Player 1 cannot improve their payoff by switching
          from <m>\tau</m> to <m>\beta</m> when Player 2 plays <m>\tau</m>.
          Start by filling in the table below for the strategy profile
          <m>(\beta, \tau)</m>.
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell>round 0</cell><cell>round 1</cell><cell>round 2</cell><cell>round 3</cell><cell>round 4</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1's move</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 2's move</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1's payoff</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
    </task>

  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-tit-for-tat-nash-not-spne">
    <title>Tit for Tat Is a Nash Equilibrium, but Not Subgame Perfect</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider again the repeated Prisoner's Dilemma from
        <xref ref="hw-trigger-and-tit-for-tat"/>, with the same stage game,
        the same discount factor <m>\delta</m>, and the same strategies.
        Suppose Player 2 uses tit for tat, <m>\tau</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <xref ref="hw-tit-for-tat-equilibrium"/> checked two ways that
        Player 1 could switch away from <m>\tau</m>: suspicious tit for tat
        and always defecting. In this exercise, we check <em>every</em> way,
        and show that <m>(\tau, \tau)</m> is a Nash equilibrium when
        <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{8}</m>. Then we show that it is not
        subgame perfect.
      </p>
      <p>
        Suppose Player 1 is cooperating along with Player 2, and then first
        defects in round <m>k</m>. Player 1 keeps defecting through round
        <m>k + m - 1</m>, and then plays <m>c</m> again in round
        <m>k + m</m>, where <m>m \geq 1</m>. We'll call this an
        <term>excursion</term>. In round <m>k + m + 1</m>, Player 1
        cooperates again, just like at the start of the game. (If Player 1
        defects again later, that's a new excursion.)
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-tit-for-tat-nash-not-spne-table">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The table below shows both players' moves during an excursion. Fill
          in Player 1's payoff (with discount factor) in each round. The last
          row is filled in for you: it is Player 1's payoff (with discount
          factor) in each round if they had kept using <m>\tau</m>, as in
          <xref ref="hw-trigger-and-tit-for-tat"/>.
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell>round 0</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell><cell>round <m>k-1</m></cell><cell>round <m>k</m></cell><cell>round <m>k+1</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell><cell>round <m>k+m-1</m></cell><cell>round <m>k+m</m></cell><cell>round <m>k+m+1</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1's move</cell><cell colspan="3"><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 2's move (<m>\tau</m>)</cell><cell colspan="3"><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1's payoff (with discount factor)</cell><cell colspan="3"></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1's payoff (with discount factor) if they had kept using <m>\tau</m></cell><cell colspan="3"><m>5, 5\delta, \ldots, 5\delta^{k-1}</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^k</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^{k+1}</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^{k+m-1}</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^{k+m}</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^{k+m+1}</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-tit-for-tat-nash-not-spne-inequality">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using your table, show that the excursion does not improve Player
          1's payoff in rounds <m>k</m> through <m>k + m</m> when
          <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{8}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Add up Player 1's payoffs in rounds <m>k</m> through <m>k + m</m>
          for the excursion, and for <m>\tau</m>. The excursion doesn't help
          if its total is at most the total for <m>\tau</m>:
          <me>
            8\delta^k + 0 + \cdots + 0 - 8\delta^{k+m} \leq 5\delta^k + 5\delta^{k+1} + \cdots + 5\delta^{k+m}.
          </me>
          After canceling a factor of <m>\delta^k</m>, this becomes
          <me>
            8 - 8\delta^m \leq 5(1 + \delta + \delta^2 + \cdots + \delta^m).
          </me>
          So you need to show that this is true when
          <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{8}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some facts that may help: the right side is a finite geometric
          series,
          <m>5(1 + \delta + \cdots + \delta^m) = \frac{5}{1 - \delta}(1 - \delta^{m+1})</m>.
          When <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{8}</m>, you know
          <m>\frac{5}{1 - \delta} \geq 8</m>. Also,
          <m>\delta^{m+1} \leq \delta^m</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-tit-for-tat-nash-not-spne-reset">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now we put the pieces together. Any way Player 1 can switch away
          from <m>\tau</m> is made of excursions, one after another, possibly
          ending with Player 1 defecting at some point and never playing
          <m>c</m> again.
        </p>
        <p>
          Look at round <m>k + m + 1</m> in the table: both players play
          <m>c</m>, just like in round 0. Since Player 2's move only depends
          on what Player 1 did in the round before, from round
          <m>k + m + 1</m> on, the game looks exactly like it did at the
          start, just with every payoff multiplied by
          <m>\delta^{k+m+1}</m>.
        </p>
        <ol marker="i.">
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>Several excursions.</em> If Player 1 switches away from
              <m>\tau</m> again later, that's a new excursion, and part (b)
              applies to it too. Explain, without computing anything, why
              Player 1 does <em>worse</em> with several excursions than by
              cooperating the whole time with <m>\tau</m>, when
              <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{8}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>Never coming back.</em> Player 1 might also defect in round
              <m>k</m> and never play <m>c</m> again (they keep defecting
              forever). Which part of
              <xref ref="hw-tit-for-tat-equilibrium"/> already showed that this
              doesn't help when <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{8}</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Putting these together: when <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{8}</m>, any
          way of switching away from <m>\tau</m> is some excursions, none of
          which help Player 1, possibly followed by defecting forever, which
          doesn't help either. So no strategy gives Player 1 a higher payoff
          against <m>\tau</m> than <m>\tau</m> does. By symmetry, the same is
          true for Player 2. So <m>(\tau, \tau)</m> is a Nash equilibrium
          when <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{8}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          For i: compare Player 1's payoffs with <m>\tau</m> one excursion at
          a time. For ii: from round <m>k</m> on, defecting forever is just
          <m>\beta</m> against <m>\tau</m>, with every payoff multiplied by
          <m>\delta^k</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-tit-for-tat-nash-not-spne-subgame">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\zeta</m> denote <term>generous tit for tat</term>: play
          <m>c</m> in rounds 0 and 1. After that, play whatever the other
          player played in the previous round. This strategy is called
          generous because it does not give up on cooperation if the other
          player begins with <m>d</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now we show that <m>(\tau, \tau)</m> is not a subgame perfect Nash
          equilibrium. Here is the setup:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              In round 0, Player 1 played <m>c</m> and Player 2 played
              <m>d</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Consider the subgame starting in round 1.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Player 2 uses <m>\tau</m> in this subgame.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              In this subgame, round 1 is discounted by <m>\delta</m>, round
              2 by <m>\delta^2</m>, and so on.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <ol marker="i.">
          <li>
            <p>
              Suppose Player 1 also uses <m>\tau</m>. Fill in the table below,
              and find Player 1's payoff in this subgame.
            </p>
            <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
              <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
              <col right="minor"/>
              <col right="minor"/>
              <col right="minor"/>
              <col right="minor"/>
              <col/>
              <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                <cell></cell><cell><line>round 1</line><line>(after <m>(c, d)</m>)</line></cell><cell>round 2</cell><cell>round 3</cell><cell>round 4</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Player 1's move (<m>\tau</m>)</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Player 2's move (<m>\tau</m>)</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Player 1's payoff (with discount factor)</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
              </row>
            </tabular>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Suppose Player 1 uses <m>\zeta</m> instead. Fill in the table
              below, and find Player 1's payoff in this subgame.
            </p>
            <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
              <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
              <col right="minor"/>
              <col right="minor"/>
              <col right="minor"/>
              <col right="minor"/>
              <col/>
              <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                <cell></cell><cell><line>round 1</line><line>(after <m>(c, d)</m>)</line></cell><cell>round 2</cell><cell>round 3</cell><cell>round 4</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Player 1's move (<m>\zeta</m>)</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Player 2's move (<m>\tau</m>)</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Player 1's payoff (with discount factor)</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
              </row>
            </tabular>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              For which values of <m>\delta</m> is Player 1's payoff larger
              with <m>\zeta</m> than with <m>\tau</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              For those values of <m>\delta</m>, is the restriction of
              <m>(\tau, \tau)</m> to this subgame a Nash equilibrium? Is
              <m>(\tau, \tau)</m> a subgame perfect Nash equilibrium? (This
              should be quick, since you just did the relevant computations.)
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          In each round, a player using <m>\tau</m> copies what the other
          player did in the round before. So in round 1, look back at round 0,
          where Player 1 played <m>c</m> and Player 2 played <m>d</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          For i, both players use <m>\tau</m>, so in round 1, Player 1 copies
          Player 2's <m>d</m> and Player 2 copies Player 1's <m>c</m>. After
          that, they keep copying each other, so they take turns defecting:
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell><line>round 1</line><line>(after <m>(c, d)</m>)</line></cell><cell>round 2</cell><cell>round 3</cell><cell>round 4</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1's move (<m>\tau</m>)</cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 2's move (<m>\tau</m>)</cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>d</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1's payoff (with discount factor)</cell><cell><m>8\delta</m></cell><cell><m>-8\delta^2</m></cell><cell><m>8\delta^3</m></cell><cell><m>-8\delta^4</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          For ii, <m>\zeta</m> plays <m>c</m> in round 1 no matter what, and
          Player 2 copies Player 1's <m>c</m> from round 0. Both cooperate in
          round 1, and then they keep copying each other's <m>c</m>:
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell><line>round 1</line><line>(after <m>(c, d)</m>)</line></cell><cell>round 2</cell><cell>round 3</cell><cell>round 4</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1's move (<m>\zeta</m>)</cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 2's move (<m>\tau</m>)</cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1's payoff (with discount factor)</cell><cell><m>5\delta</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^2</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^3</m></cell><cell><m>5\delta^4</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          Now add up each payoff row using a geometric series.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-common-fund">
    <title>Contributing to a Common Fund</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose 4 students from the class play the following game:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Each student is given one (infinitely divisible) dollar. Each
            student chooses, simultaneously, how much of their dollar to put
            into a common fund.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The amount in the common fund is multiplied by 3, and the total
            is split equally among the 4 students.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Let <m>x_i</m> denote Player <m>i</m>'s strategy, the amount they
            put into the fund, so <m>0 \leq x_i \leq 1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-common-fund-payoff">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the payoff function <m>\pi_i(x_i, x_{-i})</m> for Player
          <m>i</m>. Remember that each player receives $1 before choosing how
          much to put into the common fund.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\pi_i(x_i, x_{-i}) = 1 - x_i + \frac{3}{4}(x_1 + x_2 + x_3 + x_4)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-common-fund-dominated">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that for each player <m>i</m>, choosing <m>x_i \gt 0</m> is
          strictly dominated by choosing <m>x_i = 0</m>. In other words,
          explain why your expression from the previous part is largest when
          <m>x_i = 0</m>, no matter what the other players contribute.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-common-fund-three-payoffs">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>0 \lt y \leq 1</m>. Using your payoff function, find
          Player <m>i</m>'s payoff in each of these situations:
        </p>
        <ol marker="i.">
          <li>
            <p>Every player, including Player <m>i</m>, contributes <m>y</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Player <m>i</m> contributes 0, and every other player
              contributes <m>y</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Every player contributes 0.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-common-fund-trigger">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now suppose the same 4 students play this game over and over, in
          rounds <m>k = 0, 1, 2, \ldots</m>, with discount factor
          <m>\delta</m>, where <m>0 \lt \delta \lt 1</m>. If <m>p_k</m>
          denotes a player's payoff in round <m>k</m>, then that player's
          payoff for the repeated game is
          <me>
            \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \delta^k p_k = p_0 + \delta p_1 + \delta^2 p_2 + \cdots.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Each round is a fresh copy of the game above. At the start of every
          round, each player receives a <em>new</em> dollar and decides how
          much of that dollar to put into the common fund. Money a player
          kept or received in earlier rounds cannot be put into the fund. So
          in every round, each player's payoff is given by your formula from
          the first part.
        </p>
        <p>
          Define a <q>trigger-type</q> strategy <m>\sigma_y</m>, where
          <m>0 \lt y \leq 1</m>, as follows:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>Contribute <m>y</m> in round 0.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Keep contributing <m>y</m> in each later round, as long as
              every student, including yourself, contributed at least
              <m>y</m> in the previous round.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If one or more students (including yourself) contributed less
              than <m>y</m> in the previous round, contribute 0 in the current round, and keep
              contributing 0 in every round after that.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Find <m>\delta_0</m> such that the strategy profile
          <m>(\sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y)</m> is a Nash
          equilibrium whenever <m>\delta \geq \delta_0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>You will need your results from the previous part.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Just as with the Prisoner's Dilemma, you can think about the
              first round in which Player <m>i</m> contributes 0 instead of
              <m>y</m>. It is enough to consider the case where Player
              <m>i</m> does this right away, in round 0. What is Player
              <m>i</m>'s payoff in round 0, with everyone else contributing
              <m>y</m>? What happens in later rounds? Don't forget that the
              players keep receiving $1 in each round, even if they
              contribute 0 to the fund.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-common-fund-subgame">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now we check whether <m>(\sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y)</m>
          is a subgame perfect Nash equilibrium when
          <m>\delta \geq \delta_0</m>, just as we did for the trigger strategy
          in the Wine Merchant game. Take any subgame, starting at a node
          <m>m</m> at the beginning of round <m>k</m>. There are two cases:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Every student contributed at least <m>y</m> in every round
              before <m>m</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              At least one student contributed less than <m>y</m> in some
              round before <m>m</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <ol marker="i.">
          <li>
            <p>
              Consider a subgame where every student contributed at least
              <m>y</m> in every round before <m>m</m>. Is the restriction of
              <m>(\sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y)</m> to this subgame
              a Nash equilibrium?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Consider a subgame where at least one student contributed less
              than <m>y</m> in some round before <m>m</m>. Is the restriction
              of <m>(\sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y)</m> to this
              subgame a Nash equilibrium?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Is <m>(\sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y, \sigma_y)</m> a subgame
              perfect Nash equilibrium?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          This argument follows the same structure as
          <xref ref="example-trigger-spne"/>, where we showed that the trigger
          strategy in the Wine Merchant game is subgame perfect.
        </p>
        <p>
          For i: what does <m>\sigma_y</m> tell everyone to do at <m>m</m>?
          How does the subgame compare with the whole game? For ii: what does
          <m>\sigma_y</m> tell everyone to do in every round of the subgame?
          Use part (b).
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</exercises>
